      "side": 1,
      "message": "I do not believe the gemini feedback is accurate\n\n```\n\tif (res) {\n\t\ttimestamps_invalid |\u003d FPSTATS_CAPTURE_INV;\n\t\treturn;\n\t}\n```\n\nif `fp_process_finger` fails, due to image not being acquired, we invalidate the stats and return. We need to get a new `Finger Down` event at the next finger capture. The framework latency requires a signal whenever we try to capture a new image, which happens when we start `fp_process_finger`. This is also when our fpmcu latency starts its timer \n\n```\nstatic void fp_process_finger(void)\n{\n\ttimestamp_t t0 \u003d get_time();\n\t\n```\n\nIf the image quality is bad does not cause the capture to return with an error, the matcher returns with not authenticated. This is also give the framework latency measurement the signal to stop the measurement. \n\nThe difference between fpmcu and framework latency is that the framework get the signal from the hal when matching outcome is returned vs. fpmcu gets it from the matcher. The extra overhead comes from the overhead to the event listerner, debug (is enabled), etc. When I measured the latency difference it was around 50 msec with no debug enabled and 300 msec when debug was enabled.",
